    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Part 12: Plotly\"\nauthor: Kerry Back, Rice University\nexecute:\n  echo: true\n  jupyter: python3\n  cache: false\nformat:\n  revealjs:\n    highlight-style: github\n    code-fold: false\n    code-copy: hover\n    scrollable: true\n    slide-number: true\n    preview-links: true\n    self-contained: true\n    controls: true\n    transition: fade\n    theme: [league]\n    incremental: false\n    code-line-numbers: false\n---\n\n\n## Interactive HTML Plots\n\nPlotly creates interactive visualizations that allow users to zoom, pan, hover for details, and explore data dynamically. This can be powerful for presentations.\n\nTo create this, we can:\n\n1. Create the plotly figure\n2. Use `fig.write_html` to write it to an HTML file\n3. Copy the HTML file into the directory of your presentation\n4. Use Insert → Links → Link → Browse and select your HTML file to embed a link in the presentation\n\nThis creates a clickable link within your presentation that opens the HTML file in your default browser.\n\n## Examples of Plotly Features\n\n**Hover data:** Show details when hovering over data points - great for explaining differences between categories\n\n**Hide/Show plot elements:** Click on legend items to hide/show elements - useful for building up explanations step by step\n\n**Animation:** Show how data evolves over time with play button controls\n\n**Pan/Zoom:** All plotly figures include control elements at the top right for panning, zooming, and other interactions\n\n## Wages Data\n\nWe'll use the wages data to demonstrate Plotly visualizations.\n\n::: {#502539b3 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport pandas as pd\nimport plotly.express as px\n\nurl = 'https://faculty.utrgv.edu/diego.escobari/teaching/Datasets/WAGE1.xls'\nwages = pd.read_excel(url, header=None)\n```\n:::\n\n\n## Preparing the Data (1)\n\n::: {#3f5cb920 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncolumns = [\n    'wage', 'educ', 'exper',\n    'tenure', 'nonwhite', 'female',\n    'married', 'numdep',\n    'smsa', 'northcen',\n    'south', 'west', 'construc',\n    'ndurman', 'trcommpu', 'trade',\n    'services', 'profserv',\n    'profocc', 'clerocc', 'servocc',\n    'lwage', 'expersq', 'tenursq'\n]\nwages.columns = columns\n```\n:::\n\n\n## Preparing the Data (2)\n\n::: {#281712ab .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n# Convert binary variables to categorical\nwages['Gender'] = wages['female'].map({0: 'Male', 1: 'Female'})\nwages['Race'] = wages['nonwhite'].map({0: 'White', 1: 'Non-white'})\nwages['Marital_Status'] = wages['married'].map({0: 'Not Married', 1: 'Married'})\nwages['Urban'] = wages['smsa'].map({0: 'Rural', 1: 'Urban'})\n```\n:::\n\n\n## Preparing the Data (3)\n\n::: {#3220c584 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n# Create region categories\ndef get_region(row):\n    if row['northcen'] == 1:\n        return 'North Central'\n    elif row['south'] == 1:\n        return 'South'\n    elif row['west'] == 1:\n        return 'West'\n    else:\n        return 'Northeast'\n\nwages['Region'] = wages.apply(get_region, axis=1)\n```\n:::\n\n\n## Preparing the Data (4)\n\n::: {#c486a0e8 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n# Create industry categories\ndef get_industry(row):\n    if row['construc'] == 1:\n        return 'Construction'\n    elif row['ndurman'] == 1:\n        return 'Non-durable Manufacturing'\n    elif row['trcommpu'] == 1:\n        return 'Transportation/Communications'\n    elif row['trade'] == 1:\n        return 'Trade'\n    elif row['services'] == 1:\n        return 'Services'\n    elif row['profserv'] == 1:\n        return 'Professional Services'\n    else:\n        return 'Other'\n\nwages['Industry'] = wages.apply(get_industry, axis=1)\n```\n:::\n\n\n## Preparing the Data (5)\n\n::: {#a0f51afd .cell execution_count=6}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n# Create occupation categories\ndef get_occupation(row):\n    if row['profocc'] == 1:\n        return 'Professional'\n    elif row['clerocc'] == 1:\n        return 'Clerical'\n    elif row['servocc'] == 1:\n        return 'Service'\n    else:\n        return 'Other'\n\nwages['Occupation'] = wages.apply(get_occupation, axis=1)\n```\n:::\n\n\n## Preparing the Data (6)\n\n::: {#d099e03c .cell execution_count=7}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n# Create education and experience categories\nwages['Education_Level'] = pd.cut(wages['educ'],\n                                 bins=[0, 12, 16, 25],\n                                 labels=['High School or Less', 'Some College', 'College Plus'])\n\nwages['Experience_Level'] = pd.cut(wages['exper'],\n                                  bins=[0, 5, 15, 60],\n                                  labels=['Low (0-5)', 'Mid (6-15)', 'High (16+)'])\n\n# Drop unnecessary columns\nwages = wages.drop(columns=wages.columns[4:-9])\n\n# Create combined Gender_Race column\nwages['Gender_Race'] = [\n    x + ' ' + y for x, y in zip(wages['Gender'], wages['Race'])\n]\n```\n:::\n\n\n## Creating a Plotly Box Plot\n\n::: {#e87c6611 .cell execution_count=8}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfig = px.box(wages,\n            x='Education_Level',\n            y='wage',\n            title='Wage Distribution by Gender and Race',\n            labels={'Gender_Race': 'Demographic Group',\n                   'wage': 'Hourly Wage ($)',\n                   'Education_Level': ''},\n            template='plotly_white',\n            color='Gender_Race')\n\nfig.update_layout(\n    height=400,\n    xaxis_tickangle=-45,\n)\nfig.show()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n```{=html}\n<div>                            <div id=\"fceb0089-e32a-47f6-9ba1-435813ebbdf3\" class=\"plotly-graph-div\" style=\"height:400px; width:100%;\"></div>            <script type=\"text/javascript\">                require([\"plotly\"], function(Plotly) {                    window.PLOTLYENV=window.PLOTLYENV || {};                                    if (document.getElementById(\"fceb0089-e32a-47f